ps://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2206.07525v1)\nciteturn4search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The paper's key innovation is the development of a graph-theoretic analogue of homotopy theory. ... A connected graph $G$G is called simply connected if any two walks with the same endpoints and same parity are homotopy equivalent - the discrete analogue of topological simple connectivity. ... The core technical theorem states that if $G$G is simply connected, $\\varphi: G \\to H$φ:G→H is a $C_{2r+1}$C 2 r+1​-free homomorphism, and some odd cycle $C$C in $G$G has a small image ($|\\varphi(V(C))| \\leq 2r+2$∣φ(V(C))∣≤2 r+2), then $G$G has bounded chromatic number ($\\chi(G) < 8r^2$χ(G)<8 r 2).\nFor a graph $H$H, two edges are related if they belong to a common 4-cycle. The equivalence class $[e]^\\diamond_H$[e]H⋄​ captures how edges are \"topologically connected\" through 4-cycles. The paper then defines intersection invariants that count (modulo 2) how many edges from a walk fall into certain $\\varphi$φ-stable sets, where $\\varphi: G \\to H$φ:G→H is a graph homomorphism. These invariants are preserved under homotopy equivalence.\n\nA connected graph $G$G is called simply connected if any two walks with the same endpoints and same parity are homotopy equivalent - the discrete analogue of topological simple connectivity.\n\n## Main Technical Result\n\nThe core technical theorem states that if $G$G is simply connected, $\\varphi: G \\to H$φ:G→H is a $C_{2r+1}$C 2 r+1​-free homomorphism, and some odd cycle $C$C in $G$G has a small image ($|\\varphi(V(C))| \\leq 2r+2$∣φ(V(C))∣≤2 r+2), then $G$G has bounded chromatic number ($\\chi(G) < 8r^2$χ(G)<8 r 2).\n\nThe proof involves several sophisticated steps:\n\n  1. Reduction via intersection invariants: Using the simply connected property and intersection invariants, the chromatic number of $G$G can be bounded by the chromatic number of a specific $C_4$C 4​-closure subgraph of $H$H.\n\n  2. Analysis of short odd cycles: In $C_{2r+1}$C 2 r+1​-free graphs, if $C$C is a shortest odd cycle and $f$f is an edge of $C$C, then every edge in the $C_4$C 4​-closure $[f]^\\diamond_H$[f]H⋄​ lies on a short odd cycle that intersects $C$C.\n\n  3. Local chromatic bounds: If a $C_{2r+1}$C 2 r+1​-free graph has a central vertex within distance $r$r of all other vertices, then its chromatic number is at most $4r$4 r.\n\nThe combination of these results yields the bound $\\chi(G) < 8r^2$χ(G)<8 r 2.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring constants · oai-math-revised (https://mathcommunity.github.io/oai-math-revised/papers/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/)\nciteturn4search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 days ago; The scope note says the formalized statement is this paper's Theorem 1.1 (sharp constant for $1/2<\\gamma<3/2$, attained by $(r+1)\\mathrm{sech}^2(rx)$, no finite spectral cutoff), and links the comparator statement `LiebThirring.lean`, where `OAI.SharpLiebThirring.sharp_lieb_thirring` is stated with `sorry` as the challenge statement. ...   * Lukas Schimmer, The state of the Lieb--Thirring conjecture (arXiv:2203.06051, 2022).\n\n# Sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring constants\n\nOpenAI · September 23, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe resolve affirmatively the remaining cases of the scalar one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring conjecture: for every $\\frac12\\lt \\gamma\\lt \\frac32$, the optimal constant is the one-bound-state constant. The estimate holds for every nonnegative potential in $L^{\\gamma+1/2}(\\mathbb R)$, with all negative eigenvalues included.\n\nPDF of the manuscript\n\nIf the PDF does not display here, open it directly: Open PDF.\n\n## Notes\n\nNo notes yet. Add a reference, a comment or your name with the buttons above, or edit NOTES.md on GitHub.\n\n## Formalization\n\n  * OpenAI, Sharp finite-matrix Lieb–Thirring inequalities and all equality cases: Lean scope notes (`lean/docs/262.md`, OpenAI Math Release, 2026-10-06). The scope note says the formalized statement is this paper's Theorem 1.1 (sharp constant for $1/2<\\gamma<3/2$, attained by $(r+1)\\mathrm{sech}^2(rx)$, no finite spectral cutoff), and links the comparator statement `LiebThirring.lean`, where `OAI.SharpLiebThirring.sharp_lieb_thirring` is stated with `sorry` as the challenge statement. J. Math. per arXiv comments). Studies existence and compactness of finite-rank optimizers and identifies KdV $N$-solitons at $\\gamma=3/2$ in 1D, relevant to the paper's remark that it exhibits an equality potential \"without classifying all optimizers\". Not in the bibliography.\n  * Lukas Schimmer, The state of the Lieb--Thirring conjecture (arXiv:2203.06051, 2022). A survey of known results on the conjectured optimal constants, which gives the pre-2026 status of the cases this paper claims to resolve. Not in the bibliography.\n  * Rupert L. Frank, The Lieb-Thirring inequalities: Recent results and open problems (arXiv:2007.09326, 2020-07-18). A survey of open problems that include the one-dimensional sharp-constant conjecture for $1/2<\\gamma<3/2$ and the one-particle comparison addressed in Theorem 1.1. Not in the bibliography.\n  * Rupert L. Frank, Ari Laptev, Timo Weidl, Schrödinger Operators: Eigenvalues and Lieb–Thirring Inequalities (Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 200, 2022). A monograph on the sharp-constant results that the history section reviews (Hundertmark–Lieb–Thomas, Laptev–Weidl, Hundertmark–Laptev–Weidl). Not in the bibliography.\n\n## Lean formalization\n\nOpenAI provides Lean files for this family. The scope note, lean/docs/262.md, says which results they cover.\n\nEach result is stated in a file in `lean/ComparatorChallenges/`. These files end in `sorry` on purpose: they only state the result. The proof is in a separate module, and Lean's comparator is the tool for checking that the module proves exactly what the statement file says.\n\n  * Sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequality: statement, proof. Comparator run of 2026-10-26: accepted (log).\n\nThe comparator runs are described, with what they show and what they do not, in docs/LEAN_CHECKS.md.\n\nNobody has checked whether the Lean statements say what the paper claims.\n\n## Family 262: Sharp finite-matrix Lieb–Thirring inequalities and all equality cases\n\nProves the sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequality for $1/2\\lt \\gamma\\lt 3/2$ and arbitrary finite-matrix potentials W ≥ 0 with $\\int\\mathop{\\mathrm{tr}}\\nolimits (W^{\\gamma+1/2})\\lt \\infty$: the optimal constant is the scalar one-bound-state value, independent of matrix size. All equality cases are direct sums, in one constant unitary basis, of scalar sech^{2} solitons with independent scales and centers, and zero channels.\n\nOther papers in this family:\n\n  * Equality cases in the sharp one-dimensional matrix Lieb–Thirring inequality · October 5, 2026\n  * Sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequalities for matrix potentials · October 5, 2026\n\nManuscripts, family summaries and abstracts: OpenAI, openai/math (Apache 2.0). Notes and revisions: contributors to mathcommunity/oai-math-revised.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Lieb–Thirring conjecture for γ=1 in three dimensions — Map of Proofs in Physics (https://physicsproofs.org/theorem?id=physics-open-problems-028&view=%3Ftopic%3Dspectral-theory)\nciteturn4search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last week; Schimmer, “The state of the Lieb–Thirring conjecture,” arXiv:2203.06051 (2022). ... Schimmer, “The state of the Lieb–Thirring conjecture,” arXiv:2203.06051 (2022)..\n\n### Related questions in this collection\n\n  * #027 · The optimal Lieb–Oxford constant\n\nOriginal question ↗Source version ↗Collection credits →\n\n  * Physics Open ProblemsSources and further reading\n  * Physics Open Problems #028: The Lieb–Thirring