Li Li, “On the two-dimensional Jacobian conjecture: Magnus' formula revisited, II”, arXiv:2205.12792 (2022).\n\n# The remainder vanishing conjecture for Jacobian pairs\n\nAbout 4 years old · traced to 2022\n\nLet\n\n$$\\mathcal{Q}=\\{(a,b,m,n)\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{>0}^4:m<n,\\ a\\mid m,\\ a\\mid n,\\ \\gcd(a,b)=1,\\ 2\\leq a<b\\}.$$Q={(a,b,m,n)∈Z>0 4​:m<n, a∣m, a∣n, g cd(a,b)=1, 2≤a<b}.\n\nFor $F\\in\\mathbb{C}[x,y]$F∈C[x,y], let $N^0(F)$N 0(F) be its augmented Newton polygon, let $T_{m,n}$T m,n​ be the corresponding trapezoid, and let $F_i^{(0,1)}$F i(0,1)​ and $F_i^{(1,1)}$F i(1,1)​ denote its homogeneous components for the indicated gradings. Suppose $(a,b,m,n)\\in\\mathcal{Q}$(a,b,m,n)∈Q and $F,G\\in\\mathbb{C}[x,y]$F,G∈C[x,y] satisfy the five conditions in the conjecture: the specified trapezoidal supports for $F$F and $G$G, the $(x+1)$(x+1)- and $(x+y)$(x+y)-divisibility conditions on the components of $F$F, and $[F,G]\\in\\mathbb{C}$[F,G]∈C.\n\nThe remainder vanishing conjecture. Under these conditions,\n\n$$[F,G]=0.$$[F,G]=0.\n\nThe paper names this conjecture and states that it implies the Newton-polygon criterion, hence the two-dimensional Jacobian conjecture. Its resolution status is not supplied in the source material.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nJacob Glidewell, William E. Hurst, Kyungyong Lee and Li Li, “On the two-dimensional Jacobian conjecture: Magnus' formula revisited, II”, arXiv:2205.12792 (2022).\n\ncluster-algebrasjacobian-conjecturenewton-polygons\n\nPosted 2 months ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncphysics.org (https://www.cphysics.org/article/28477.pdf)\nciteturn4search12 [wordli